observer (0.11.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A coloured dot next to every machine in the list says whether it is
    reachable, without clicking on it: grey until checked, green when it
    answers, amber while a fault is younger than ten seconds, red after
    that. It is the same rule as the status bar, so a red dot means what a
    red bar means; a rejected token or an incompatible version is red at
    once. Hovering the dot, or a screen reader, gives the reason in words.
  * The machines you are not looking at are probed every fifteen seconds,
    all together and without holding up the gauges: a machine that is off
    costs an eight-second timeout, and the gauges must not pay it.
  * The rows of the machine list carry their accessible name themselves,
    where keyboard navigation and screen readers actually look.
  * A machine that is not being watched and whose token or fingerprint has
    been changed on disk is read again the next time its probe is refused,
    so its dot recovers without restarting the dashboard.

 -- Federico Cardinali <noreply@example.invalid>  Thu, 03 Sep 2026 16:30:00 +0000

observer (0.10.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The gauges now fill the row: as many columns as fit at the smallest
    readable size, then every gauge grows - up to half again as large - to
    use the width there is. No more holes at the end of a row.
  * The machine list is as wide as the names in it, between 150 and 240
    pixels, instead of a fixed 210 that spent a third of a small window on
    empty space.
  * The window remembers where it was, how big, and whether it was
    maximised, and reopens there - unless that place is on a screen that is
    no longer connected, in which case it opens where the system puts it.
  * A "Text size" setting in the top right corner, with the same steps as
    Windows (100, 115, 130, 150 %): it scales the whole window, gauges
    included, and is remembered. The minimum window size grows with it, so
    the layout never breaks. It exists because the toolkit does not read
    the system text size.
  * Both are kept in preferences.json next to client.json - a separate file,
    because client.json may carry a credential and should not be rewritten
    at every close.

